# CODEOWNERS — required review by domain.
#
# GitHub only enforces this once branch protection on `main` has
# "Require review from Code Owners" enabled (see BRANCH_PROTECTION.md)
# AND every handle below resolves to a real GitHub identity. The previous
# version of this file referenced @ai-training-academy/runtime-owners and
# three sibling teams — those don't exist. This repository belongs to the
# personal account @sthompson35, not an organization, and personal accounts
# cannot own teams. Unrecognized handles are silently ignored by GitHub, not
# an error, so that version of the file was never actually enforcing
# anything, even with the setting turned on.
#
# Known structural conflict, not yet resolved by this file alone:
# with a single owner (@sthompson35) as both the author of most PRs and the
# code owner required to review them, "require code owner review" +
# "required_approving_review_count: 1" together reproduce the exact
# self-approval deadlock PR #2 hit — GitHub does not count self-approval
# toward either requirement. This file alone does not fix that; one of the
# following has to happen before branch protection is tightened back up:
#   (a) add a second real GitHub collaborator who can review, or
#   (b) keep required_approving_review_count at 0 for solo operation and
#       rely on required CI + required conversation resolution instead,
#       raising it to 1 once (a) happens.
# See BRANCH_PROTECTION.md's "Solo-maintainer note" for the tradeoff — this
# file intentionally does not pick one for you.
#
# Later patterns win on overlap, matching GitHub's own precedence rule —
# most specific path last.

